{[%evp 0,20,21,14,11,11,1,3,32767,32767,5,0,3,0,13,32767,32767,32767,27,-11,0,-6,-3] Welcome to Chapter 27, where we will explore the London System. The London System has surged in popularity as players have struggled to find new ideas in the main lines. Full disclosure: the London System is a respectable opening where white can aim for meaningful play. However, there are two types of London System players currently:   A) Players who are ambitious and approach the London System with good preparation.   B) Players who prefer a fixed setup and want a straightforward game.  The first category of players isn't a concern, as they are eager to fight. However, the second category of players, typically club-level London players, who just want to execute a d4 - Bf4 - e3 - Bd3 - Nbd2 - c3 sequence in some order and play a game, are more concerning. The recommendation in this chapter is specifically aimed at these players, pushing them into situations where they need to be precise and navigate sharper positions.  Just like the previous chapters, we will begin with the main line and follow up with sidelines and alternatives in subsequent chapters.} 1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 {The starting point of this chapter. White develops a piece in the opening. The drawback is that, unlike the 2.c4 lines, White's not looking to gain space in the center.} Nf6 {We develop our Knight routinely.} 3. e3 {White opens up the diagonal for the f1 -Bishop. White usually places it on d3 , or in some rare cases, b5 , when black has played c5 & Nc6 .} e6 {The best move order for our setup. Whatever White plays for the next couple of moves, we would like to play ... c5 - Nc6 whenever possible. With ... e6 , we defend the d5 -pawn and prepare ... c5 , with the defence of the f8 -Bishop. The drawback of this move is that the c8 -Bishop is shut down.} 4. Nf3 {White develops the Knight.} c5 {We gain space on the Queenside, and prepare ... Nc6 , or ... Qb6 .} 5. Nbd2 {The modern main line. White waits before committing c3 and includes the knight in the game, prioritizing development in the opening.} Nc6 {We bring out our Knight to the most active square and increase pressure over d4 . The drawback of ... Nc6 is that in some variations, White can pin our Knight with Bb5 .} 6. c3 {White reinforces the support of the d4 -pawn and maintains the option to activate their queen onto the queenside in the future. This represents the main tabiya of the London System, with at least five, if not more, reasonable options for black. I now recommend one of the most strategically complex systems.} cxd4 {We want the e-pawn to release the support of the f4 -Bishop.} 7. exd4 Nh5 {This is one of the major main lines in the London System, but it does not offer an easy path for White. They must either enter into complications or play a strategically complex position, both of which are favorable outcomes for us in terms of opening strategy. With ... Nh5 , we push the Bishop away from the f4 diagonal, and our plan is to bring our f8 -Bishop to d6 , where it will be ideally placed.} 8. Be3 {The main move, and the point of study in this chapter. White retreats with the Bishop to the safest square.} Bd6 {We bring our Bishop to the most active square. We have an exchange Caro pawn structure on the board, but there are subtle differences in our favor. Our Bishop on d6 is ideally placed, which we cannot achieve in the exchange Caro. We also maintain control of the e5 -square. The only drawback is that our knight on h5 is vulnerable.} 9. g3 {This is a calmer approach from White's end, usually preferred by players who don't want to play too ambitiously. However, this is a 4th pawn move by White in the opening.} (9. Bd3 {White develops the bishop and brings it to the most active square.} Nf4 {Our point. We threaten g2 and d3 .} 10. Bxf4 {Which wins the bishop pair.} Bxf4 11. O-O {White finishes the kingside development.} (11. g3 Bd6 12. O-O O-O 13. Re1 {transposes. A very typical move in these structures. White wants to play h4 - Kg2 at some point.} Bd7 {We continue with our plan of development.} 14. Rc1 {White improves the rook} Rc8 {so do we. The position is quite complex strategically, but our typical plan is to play Kh8 next, and remain flexible with our plans.} 15. a3 {white is preparing c4 , and stops Nb4 .} Kh8 {Getting out of all Bxh7 ideas after Qc2 .} 16. c4 {Principled continuation, but allows} (16. b4 {Is the comp's top choice. White gains space on the Queenside, but we can play} Ne7 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.Bd3 Nf4 10.Bxf4 #6 With the idea of Ng8 - Nf6 ! We improve the position of our knight and keep pressure over the c3 -pawn. We may also try a5 , looking to attack weaknesses on the Queenside. The position is quite complex strategically, with both sides having their chances. Note that Ng5 is met by h6 , and we are not afraid of Qh5 due to Be8}) 16... Qf6 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.Bd3 Nf4 10.Bxf4 #5 where we have a comfortable game. We would look to increase pressure over d4 .}) 11... O-O {And so do we. We have a couple of plans here, namely ... f6 - Bd6 - e5 , or playing on the queenside with ... Bd7 - Rb8 - b5 . We have the pair of bishops, but white has a lead in development, along with a slightly more active positioning of pieces.} 12. Qe2 {Bringing the queen to e2 is less flexible, and we respond typically!} (12. Re1 {The most natural. White brings the rook to the semi-open file. Although this is an opening course, I want to highlight a typical strategic motif. I feel Re1 is the strongest move because you keep the maximum flexibility. I don't know if the queen belongs to e2 or c2 yet.   With Re1 , white can choose either of these based on Black's responses. My coach used to call this concept a 'necessary move'. You are free to label it according to your wishes.} Bd6 {We get our bishop back, so that we can play ... f6 - e5 later. We preempt g3 .} 13. b4 {weakens c3 . We can just play} (13. Nf1 {White looks to maneuver the knight to g3 , to bring more pieces on the kingside.} Bd7 {We develop our bishop and keep our flexibility of playing ... f6 - e5 /playing on the queenside.} 14. Ng3 {White continues with the plan and brings the knight towards the kingside.} Kh8 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.Bd3 Nf4 10.Bxf4 #3 This is a very useful move in these structures. What we do is basically get out of ... Bxh7 / Bxg6 checks, after Qc2 , and also prepare g5 in some variations. We keep the flexibility of ... f6 - e5 or ... f6 - g5 & ... Rb8 - b5 . If white plays b4 in these structures, we may also play ... Ne7 - Ng8 - Nf6 :-),}) (13. h4 {Is also not threatening. We continue with our plan with} Bd7 {and prepare Kh8 next. White cannot start attacking on the kingside with} 14. Ng5 {as after} g6 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.Bd3 Nf4 10.Bxf4 #4 We have ideas of Be7 , winning material, or e5 , gaining space in the center.} (14... f6 {is met by} 15. c4 {where we aren't able to push e5 easily.})) 13... Bd7 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.Bd3 Nf4 10.Bxf4 #2 followed by Rc8 . White has weakened c3 unnecessarily.}) 12... f6 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.Bd3 Nf4 10.Bxf4 #1 Preparing ... e5 , since ... dxe5 is always risky due to the pin on the e-file, after Re8 .}) 9... Nf6 ({Anti-intuitive, but quite strong. We are looking to win the Bishop pair with} 9... Z0 {Ng4 , as} 10. Bg5 {is met by} f6 11. Bh4 g5 {.}) 10. h3 {The most logical, yet inaccurate. White stops ... Ng4 but spends too much time in the opening with pawns, and creates a weakness on h3 .} (10. Bd3 {White develops naturally, and brings us a piece into the game.} Ng4 {Our idea. We could have castled too, but I want to create strategic imbalances and win the bishop pair. The drawback of course is that we have spent a considerable time with our knight.} 11. O-O {allows us to show our idea with} (11. Bg5 {is met} f6 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.Bd3 #2 Is basically what we want, while white is going to lose the Bishop anyways. f6 gives us an extra option of gaining space in the center with e5 .}) (11. Qe2 {The top choice of the comp. White looks to recapture on e3 with the queen, keeping the queenside pawn structure intact.} Nxe3 {We win the bishop pair nevertheless.} 12. Qxe3 {We have the Bishop pair, while white has slightly active pieces. Our goal is to finish development while wanting to exploit their lead in development.} O-O {We finish our kingside development, and want to play f6 - e5 next, gaining space in the center, and opening up the diagonal/file for the bishop/rook.} 13. h4 {Isn't approved by the computer, but looks very tempting. White wants to launch an attack with h5 / Ng5 next. However, we don't need to be afraid since Ng5 can always be met with h6 safely. These attacks are dangerous with the dark-squared Bishops in the game, but without them, we don't need to be fearful.} (13. Ng5 {Is too hasty, and we can safely play} h6 {and} 14. h4 {is met by} hxg5 15. hxg5 g6 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.Bd3 #4 With a clearly winning position. As Ian mentions in the Wch press conference, playing white would have been so much better if you can play Ng5 & h4 all the time.}) (13. O-O-O {White commits the king, so we start attacking it with} Rb8 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.Bd3 #5 followed by b5 - b4 . As usual, we are not afraid of h4 - Ng5 , and can play h6 .}) (13. O-O {The simplest approach from white. White finishes the kingside development.} f6 {We look to gain space in the centre with e5 .} 14. c4 {Stopping e5 , is the strongest move. However, this weakens d4 .} (14. b4 {allows us to display our idea with} e5 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.Bd3 #8 where we are better due to the extra space and the pair of Bishops.}) 14... Bc7 {With a very subtle threat of Bb6 , preparing e5 . We want white to play ... c5 , closing down the centre and releasing the pressure on d5 , so that we play ... Bc7 and ... e5 again.} 15. Rfe1 {A normal developing move.} Bb6 {Our point. We increase the pressure over d4 .} 16. cxd5 (16. c5 {allows} Bc7 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.Bd3 #7 we want to play e5 next, for the reasons explained earlier, gaining space and opening up the diagonal for the Bishop.}) 16... exd5 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.Bd3 #6 Opens up the diagonal for our bishop, giving us better chances of outplaying White. With the advantage of having two bishops and an easier plan and play for Black, our position looks promising.}) 13... Qb6 {We start attacking white's weaknesses.} 14. O-O-O {White has completed the development, and wants to start attacking, but we have the central breakthrough first.} (14. Ng5 {doesn't lead anywhere, and we can safely play} h6 {followed by the typical e5 .}) 14... e5 {Challenging white's center.} 15. Nxe5 {This doesn't work.} Nxe5 16. dxe5 Qxe3 {The important intermezzo.} 17. fxe3 Bxe5 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.Bd3 #3 We are better due to better pawn structure and pair of bishops.}) 11... Nxe3 {we win the Bishop pair} 12. fxe3 {White has a lead in development and our king is in the center, so white wants to open up the position with e4 . We prevent this by playing} f5 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.Bd3 #1 Followed by ... O-O . We'll look to improve our worst-placed piece, the c8 -bishop next, with Bd7 - Be8 . We have a pair of Bishops and and better pawn structure for a slight lack in development. Playing this is easier for black. All we need to do is finish our development.}) 10... O-O {We have finished our kingside development while white is yet to finish it. We look to start playing on the kingside/center.} 11. Bg2 (11. Bd3 {The best square for White's Bishop. However, this does not keep the h3 pawn guarded which we can exploit by the principled} Re8 {preparing to meet} 12. O-O {with} e5 {27) London System - Main Line-8.Be3 Bd6 9.g3 Nf6 10.h3 #2 White has some issues with the h3 -pawn. After we trade everything on e5 , we remain with extra space, better pieces as well as have a huge target on h3 . Black is clearly better.}) 11... Ne4 {We centralize the knight and prepare ... f5 next, followed by chances on the kingside.} (11... b5 $5 {playing in the minority attack fashion with b5 - a5 - b4 is quite strong too.}) *

